---
import flujoClaroSvg from "../assets/flujo-light.svg?raw";
import flujoOscuroSvg from "../assets/flujo-dark.svg?raw";

// El héroe ya incluye estos SVG: otra copia con los mismos id rompería sus marcadores de flecha.
const flujoClaro = flujoClaroSvg.replaceAll("dk-light", "dk2-light");
const flujoOscuro = flujoOscuroSvg.replaceAll("dk-dark", "dk2-dark");
---
<section class="sec" id="diagrams" aria-labelledby="t-diagrams">
  <h2 id="t-diagrams"><span class="n">2</span> Diagrams that stay true</h2>
  <p class="lede">Flows, sequences, states and data, drawn in Mermaid, the way Obsidian and GitHub render them. Each diagram carries its anchors inside, on <code>%% dockeeper:</code> lines nobody sees, so <code>check</code> flags the diagram when the code it shows changes.</p>
  <aside class="mn"><b>One rule</b>Every box is something that exists in the code, under the name it has there. No invented boxes.
    <!-- dockeeper: dockeeper/markdown.py#_fichas -->
  </aside>
  <div class="wide pair">
    <figure>
      <figcaption>What the agent writes</figcaption>
<pre class="src"><span class="fence">&#96;&#96;&#96;mermaid</span>
flowchart LR
  W["Webhook"] --> F&#123;"Valid signature?"&#125;
  <span class="pct">&#37;&#37; dockeeper: src/webhooks.ts#verifySignature</span>
  F -- no --> R["401 Unauthorized"]
  F -- yes --> P["Process the payment"]
  <span class="pct">&#37;&#37; dockeeper: src/payments.ts#process</span>
  P --> L[("ledger")]
<span class="fence">&#96;&#96;&#96;</span></pre>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <figcaption>What GitHub and Obsidian show</figcaption>
      <div class="diagram">
        <div class="light" set:html={flujoClaro} />
        <div class="dark" set:html={flujoOscuro} />
      </div>
    </figure>
  </div>
  <div class="wide kinds">
    <span>flowchart</span><span>sequenceDiagram</span><span>stateDiagram-v2</span><span>erDiagram</span><span>classDiagram</span><span>pie</span><span>timeline</span><span>&gt; [!WARNING]</span>
  </div>
</section>
